C LB+UB+data
"sMRD Example 1.1a: load buffering with a FALSE dependency masked by undefined
 behaviour. P0 reads x into r1, then stores to y the value 1/!r1 -- which is
 only DEFINED when r1==0 (otherwise it is a division by zero, i.e. UB). A
 compiler may therefore assume r1==0, constant-fold the store to a plain y=1,
 and break the read(x)->write(y) dependency. P1 reads y into r2 and stores r2 to
 x. The outcome r1=1 /\ r2=1 then becomes reachable.

 sMRD (Symbolic MRD) ALLOWS it: its symbolic dependency calculus reasons about
 undefined behaviour, sees the dependency is semantically false, removes it, and
 with no dp edge there is no (dp union rf) cycle to discard. This matches GCC/
 Clang, which perform exactly this optimisation. MRD (Modular Relaxed
 Dependencies) FORBIDS it: MRD computes dependencies over CONCRETE locations and
 values from program syntax, is UB-unaware, keeps the read(x)->write(y) data
 dependency, and so retains a (dp union rf) cycle and rules the outcome out.
 Neither model is in herd7; verdicts are CITED. See README."
{ int x=0; int y=0; }
P0 (atomic_int* x, atomic_int* y) {
  int r1 = atomic_load_explicit(x,memory_order_relaxed);
  atomic_store_explicit(y, 1/(1-r1), memory_order_relaxed);   /* the paper's 1/!r1: defined only when r1==0 */
}
P1 (atomic_int* x, atomic_int* y) {
  int r2 = atomic_load_explicit(y,memory_order_relaxed);
  atomic_store_explicit(x, r2, memory_order_relaxed);
}
exists (0:r1=1 /\ 1:r2=1)
